onvergence for the series\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}=\\frac{2x}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{2}}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{(2x)^{3}}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{(2x)^{4}}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,.\n$$\n\nThe interval of convergence is\n{{field-1}}\n$\\le x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." 13194139535284 true] [17592186341655 1113 #uuid "5c89e58c-a9b0-5e19-aea5-3e2d4756af1a" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341655 1145 "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{(-1)^{n+2}(2x)^{n+1}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}\\cdot \\frac{(n+4)^{3/2}}{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{-2x(n+4)^{3/2}}{(n+5)^{3/2}}| \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot {lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{n+4}{n+5})}^{3/2} \\\\  & =2|x|\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2|x|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied for $2|x|<1,$ or equivalently $|x|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ So, the radius of convergence is $\\frac{1}{2},$ and the series converges for any $x$ that lies in the open interval $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nThe ratio test gives no conclusion about the convergence of the series when $L=1.$ So, when $|x|=\\frac{1}{2},$ we do not know if the series converges. This means that the endpoints $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ need to be investigated separately using another test for convergence.\n\n- When $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}\\cdot (1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}-\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is convergent by the alternating series test.  So the endpoint $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\n- When $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(2x)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(-1)^{n}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{2n+1}}{(n+4)^{3/2}} \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{{5}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{6}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{7}^{3/2}}+\\frac{1}{{8}^{3/2}}+⋯)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe notice that each term of this series ${a}_{n}=\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is smaller than ${b}_{n}=\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}\\le \\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}\n$$\n\nSince ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{3/2}}$ is convergent, the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{(n+4)^{3/2}}$ is convergent by the comparison test. So the endpoint $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ is included in our interval.\n\nTherefore, the interval of convergence is $[-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}],$ or equivalently, $-\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le \\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341656 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341657 13194139535284 true] [17592186341656 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535284 true] [17592186341656 1041 "field-1" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341656 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341657 13194139535284 true] [17592186341656 1110 #uuid "d3420e66-368a-5d24-aef1-32e596cf3648" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341657 1034 "-\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341657 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139535284 true] [17592186341657 1111 #uuid "3cdf04bb-c9da-5a8a-bbad-5abbfd354f6e" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341658 1000 #edb/ref 17592186341659 13194139535284 true] [17592186341658 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139535284 true] [17592186341658 1041 "field-2" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341658 1104 #edb/ref 17592186341659 13194139535284 true] [17592186341658 1110 #uuid "e1dc98cb-51eb-56ea-94b9-660fc8f6e86e" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341659 1034 "\\le" 13194139535284 true] [17592186341659 1103 #edb/ref 17592186